ML_PAGE("l3-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l3\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 3 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Chapter 3 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 30 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with the answer, the logic and the exam trap hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Exam traps explained</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l3\" data-quiz=\"3\">Start Chapter 3 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l3\" id=\"l3p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">5 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - EXAM STRATEGY</li><li data-full=\"Your practice examination\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s2\" title=\"Your practice examination\">Your practice examination</button></li><li data-full=\"Three reasoning principles to keep in mind\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s3\" title=\"Three reasoning principles to keep in mind\">Three reasoning principles to keep in mind</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Practice questions (Q1-Q30)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s5\" title=\"Practice questions (Q1-Q30)\">Practice questions (Q1-Q30)</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART C - REVISION</li><li data-full=\"Revision sheet: the distinctions most likely to be tested\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s7\" title=\"Revision sheet: the distinctions most likely to be tested\">Revision sheet</button></li><li data-full=\"One question-selection framework\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s8\" title=\"One question-selection framework\">One question-selection framework</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l3\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 3 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Exam strategy, practice questions, formula sheets and review - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>5 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 3 - MCQ Exam Preparation</h2>\n<p>Fundamentals of Investing · Investment Information and Securities Transactions</p>\n<p>Chapter 3 presents a different challenge from Chapter 2. You may understand how stock markets operate, but can you actually make a sound investment decision and execute it correctly?</p>\n<p>This practice exam will test six areas from your Chapter 3 slides: online investing, investment information, market indexes, stockbrokers and accounts, trading orders and transaction costs, and investor protection and advisory services.</p>\n<p>The emphasis is on Bloom's Apply, Analyse and Evaluate levels, with questions that require you to judge competing alternatives rather than merely recall definitions.</p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l3p-d1\">PART A - EXAM STRATEGY</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Your practice examination</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p><strong>Questions:</strong> 30 · <strong>Suggested time:</strong> 45 min · <strong>Target score:</strong> 80%</p>\n<p>Select the single best answer. You can move backward to revise answers, submit when finished, and review your mistakes with detailed explanations and exam traps.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Three reasoning principles to keep in mind</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><ol>\n<li>\n<p>Information is not necessarily knowledge. Distinguish factual observations from forecasts, recommendations and unsupported opinions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Trading instructions involve trade-offs. Market orders prioritize execution, while limit orders prioritize price control. A stop-loss price is not a guaranteed selling price.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Judge services by their economic function. A broker executes transactions, an advisor may help with decisions, an index provides a benchmark, and investor protection does not insure you against ordinary market losses.</p>\n</li>\n</ol></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l3p-d4\">PART B - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Practice questions (Q1-Q30)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>An investor argues that online trading platforms necessarily improve investment performance because they provide faster execution, more information and lower commissions. Which response is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct, because greater access to information guarantees profitable decisions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Incorrect, because online investing always produces lower returns than traditional investing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Online tools improve accessibility, but investment outcomes still depend on decision quality, costs and risk management.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Correct, provided the investor trades frequently enough.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter recognizes the advantages of convenient research and execution but warns about unreliable advice, excessive trading, transaction costs, lack of a live broker as a safety net and margin risks. Faster trading is not the same as better investing.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish technological efficiency from the quality of an investment decision.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>A company's annual report shows that revenue increased by 12% and net income declined by 4% last year. How should this information primarily be classified?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Analytical, because it predicts future stock prices</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Descriptive, because it reports historical company performance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Speculative, because accounting statements cannot contain facts</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Advisory, because every annual report contains a recommendation to purchase shares</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Descriptive information presents factual data concerning historical behavior. Revenue and earnings figures in a company report are historical observations, rather than forecasts or recommendations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Observed financial performance is not automatically a forecast of future performance.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>An analyst observes that Company X's revenue rose 15% last year and then predicts that its shares will appreciate 25% next year. Which interpretation is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both statements are purely descriptive.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The revenue figure is analytical and the price prediction is descriptive.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both statements are guaranteed facts because an analyst made them.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The revenue figure is descriptive, while the stock-price forecast is analytical.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The historical revenue figure reports what happened. The share-price forecast uses assumptions and judgment to estimate what might happen. The chapter distinguishes descriptive data from analytical projections and recommendations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Professional forecasts remain forecasts, even if they are based on genuine financial data.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>An investor wants to inspect a U.S. public company's annual financial disclosures rather than rely solely on social media summaries. Which source from the chapter is most directly relevant?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The company's Form 10-K filing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> An anonymous investment discussion thread</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A promotional newsletter with no disclosed sources</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An unrelated market index price chart</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The chapter identifies Form 10-K as an annual filing submitted to the SEC by public companies in its U.S. context. Company filings offer direct information for verification and analysis.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Primary-source disclosure is more suitable for verifying reported financial results than unsupported commentary.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>A publicly traded company discloses material corporate information to selected professional analysts before making it available to ordinary investors. Which regulatory principle discussed in Chapter 3 is most directly relevant?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The principle that every investor must earn the same return</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The rule that all investments require identical brokerage commissions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Regulation FD's requirement concerning simultaneous public and professional disclosure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The rule that all analysts must own shares in companies they cover</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter discusses Regulation FD as requiring important company information to be disclosed simultaneously to investment professionals and the public. Its purpose relates to unequal access to material company information.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The central issue is selective disclosure, not whether investors eventually earn identical returns.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>An online promoter buys a small company's shares, publishes exaggerated claims to attract investors, and sells their holdings after the share price rises. What scheme best describes this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Ordinary portfolio rebalancing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Pump-and-dump</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> An investment club's research process</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A legitimate capital raising exercise by the issuer</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A pump-and-dump scheme involves promoting or hyping a security to inflate market demand and price, then selling into the artificially created demand. The promoter's incentives may conflict with those of later investors.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Ask who benefits when investors act on the information being promoted.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>An unlicensed promoter offers an investment promising 25% monthly returns with no possibility of loss. The investment structure is unclear. What is the most appropriate response using the chapter's investor-protection questions?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Invest quickly because the potential return compensates for missing documentation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Ignore licensing because promised returns are more important.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Assume the investment is legitimate if many people recommend it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Verify the seller, registration, risk-reward characteristics and investment mechanism before committing capital.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The chapter recommends checking whether sellers are licensed, investments are registered, risks are understood and investor recourse exists. Unusually high guaranteed returns and opacity are reasons for greater scrutiny.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> High promised return is not proof of legitimacy or low risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>Your equity portfolio earned 8% last year, while its appropriately matched market benchmark earned 20%. Which conclusion is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The portfolio earned a positive absolute return but underperformed its benchmark by 12 percentage points.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The portfolio outperformed because any positive return is automatically superior.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The portfolio must have lower risk because its return was lower.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The market benchmark is irrelevant whenever a portfolio is profitable.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>An 8% return is positive in absolute terms but 12 percentage points below the relevant benchmark's 20% return. The comparison indicates relative underperformance, although a complete evaluation should also consider risk and the portfolio's objectives.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Separate absolute performance from relative performance.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>According to the chapter, what most clearly distinguishes a stock market index from a simple market average?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> An index includes only bonds, whereas an average includes equities.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> An index measures market behavior relative to a previously established base value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> An index always guarantees a return matching inflation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Averages are always more diversified than indexes.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chapter distinguishes averages based on arithmetic price behavior from indexes reflecting values relative to a base period. Both help summarize market performance, but they use different measurement constructions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An index is a measurement of a specified securities basket, not a promise of investment performance.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>An investor wants a broader indicator of major U.S. equities instead of one limited to 30 selected large companies. Which index is more suitable under the chapter's comparison?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Dow Jones Transportation Average</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Dow Jones Utilities Average</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> S&amp;P 500 Composite Index</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Dow Jones Industrial Average</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter describes the S&amp;P 500 as covering 500 companies across major sectors and as more representative of the broad stock market than the 30-stock DJIA.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The most familiar market average is not necessarily the broadest benchmark.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>An investor wants an index in which smaller constituent companies are not overshadowed solely because other companies have larger market values. Which method best addresses that concern?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Use a portfolio weighted entirely toward the largest company.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Exclude every company with a market value below the average.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Use an index consisting of just one large stock.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Use equal weighting, as described for the Value Line Composite Index.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The chapter highlights the Value Line Composite's equal-weighting approach as a method of reducing the domination of an index by large market-value companies. The weighting method affects how index returns are measured.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Two indexes holding similar securities can behave differently if their weighting rules differ.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>An investor is studying U.S. small-company stocks and wants the most directly relevant benchmark among the indices discussed in the slides. Which one should they consider?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Russell 2000 Index</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Russell 1000 Index</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Dow Jones Utilities Average</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Dow Jones Transportation Average</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The chapter identifies the Russell 2000 as covering smaller companies and the Russell 1000 as covering larger companies. The benchmark should represent the segment being analyzed.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Always match the benchmark's investment universe to the portfolio being evaluated.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>A bond pays a fixed coupon, but an investor purchases it below its face value and intends to hold it to maturity. Why might its yield differ from its coupon rate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Coupon payments automatically change whenever the market price changes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The bond becomes ordinary equity when purchased below face value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Yield incorporates the return relative to the price paid, including the effect of maturity value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Yield must always equal the bond's original stated coupon rate.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter's bond-yield discussion emphasizes total return associated with buying a bond at its current price and holding it to maturity. The price paid matters because the investor can receive a gain or loss relative to the bond's maturity repayment.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Coupon rate and yield are not interchangeable, especially when the bond trades away from face value.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>An investor's shares are registered in the brokerage firm's name but held for the investor, who remains the beneficial owner. What arrangement is described?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Short selling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Street-name registration</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A custodial account that transfers ownership permanently</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A corporate rights offering</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chapter describes street name as securities being registered in the brokerage firm's name while the client retains beneficial ownership. It is a custody and administration arrangement, not a sale of ownership to the broker.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Legal registration of securities and beneficial economic ownership need not be in the same name.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>An experienced investor conducts independent research, makes their own investment decisions and requires only cost-efficient trade execution. Which broker type is most aligned with that need?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A full-service broker charging more for extensive advisory services</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A professional advisor with discretionary control regardless of fees</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> An investment club instead of a brokerage account</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A basic discount broker focused on transaction execution</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The chapter describes basic discount brokers as concentrating on electronic execution with little or no research or investment advice. For an investor who does not require extra advisory services, avoiding unnecessary fees is economically sensible.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Paying more for services is rational only when those services add sufficient value.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>A commission-based broker repeatedly encourages a client to buy and sell securities even though the trades serve no clear investment purpose. What is the main concern?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Churning to generate commissions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Improved diversification through every additional trade</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Regulation FD disclosure violations by the issuing company</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A guaranteed increase in after-tax returns</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The chapter warns that churning involves excessive trading of client accounts to increase commissions. More trading can benefit an intermediary while hurting the client through costs and poor decisions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Consider whether the intermediary's compensation incentives align with the client's objectives.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>An investor wishes to borrow part of the funds needed to purchase shares, using securities as collateral. Which brokerage account type is most appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A cash account, which automatically provides unsecured loans</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A custodial account for a minor</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A margin account with borrowing privileges</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A wrap account, which guarantees leveraged gains</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter identifies margin accounts as brokerage accounts that provide borrowing privileges. A cash account does not serve the same financing role; margin borrowing also introduces financing costs and magnified losses.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An account's purpose is determined by the rights and obligations it creates, not merely by which securities it can hold.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>An investor prefers to delegate investment decisions to a professional manager and pay a flat annual account fee. Which account type in the chapter best fits this arrangement?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Cash account</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Wrap account</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Odd-lot account</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Self-directed margin account</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chapter defines a wrap account as shifting investment decisions to a professional money manager in return for an annual account fee. This differs from an execution-only arrangement.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Delegating investment decisions is different from merely receiving research or execution services.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>A stock is trading actively. An investor must sell their position as quickly as reasonably possible and is willing to accept the best available execution price. Which order best matches the objective?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A buy limit order</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A good-'til-canceled buy order</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A sell limit far above the current market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A market sell order</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A market order prioritizes immediate execution at the available market price. Unlike a limit order, it does not guarantee an exact selling price and can be affected by liquidity or price changes.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Execution priority and price protection are different objectives.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>A stock is quoted around RM50. An investor wants to purchase it only if the execution price is RM47 or lower. What is the most suitable instruction?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Buy limit at RM47</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Buy market order</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sell stop at RM47</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Buy stop at RM53</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A buy limit order specifies the maximum acceptable price. The trade can execute at RM47 or below if a suitable seller is available, but execution is not guaranteed.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A buy limit sets a ceiling on the price paid, not a guaranteed future purchase.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>An investor owns shares trading near RM60 and will sell only for RM65 or more. Which instruction is most appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Market sell immediately</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Buy limit at RM65</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sell limit at RM65</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Sell stop at RM65</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A sell limit establishes a minimum acceptable selling price. An order at RM65 can fill at RM65 or higher, subject to market conditions, but may remain unfilled.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Buy limits establish maximum purchase prices; sell limits establish minimum selling prices.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>An investor submits a fill-or-kill order to purchase 500 shares at a specified limit price. Only 300 shares can be executed immediately under the order's conditions. What should happen?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The broker must purchase the other 200 shares at any price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The order is canceled rather than partially filled.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The remaining 200 shares must stay open for six months.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The order automatically becomes a stop-loss order.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chapter describes fill-or-kill orders as requiring immediate execution or cancellation. The stated quantity cannot be completely filled, so the order is canceled rather than partially executed.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse fill-or-kill with day orders or GTC instructions.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>A stock trades at RM20. The investor places a sell stop at RM17. Overnight, adverse news causes the first available trading price to fall to RM14, triggering the order. What is the most accurate conclusion?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The broker must sell at RM17 regardless of market conditions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The stop order cannot execute if the price falls below RM17.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The stop becomes a limit order at RM17.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The stop can become a market order and execute near RM14, rather than at the stop price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The chapter states that a stop order becomes a market order when activated. RM17 is the triggering level, not a guaranteed execution price. A gap down can produce substantial slippage.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Stop price is a trigger, not an insurance contract against losses beyond that level.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>An investor places a sell stop-limit order with a stop trigger of RM18 and a minimum selling limit of RM17.50. The stock gaps down to RM15 without available bids at RM17.50 or higher. What is the main risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The order activates but may remain unfilled because the price limit cannot be satisfied.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The stock must be sold at exactly RM18.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The broker must guarantee execution at RM17.50.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The instruction automatically converts into a market buy order.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A stop-limit adds execution-price protection but sacrifices certainty of execution. Once activated, the sell limit will not accept prices below RM17.50, so the investor can remain exposed if the market trades lower.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Stop-market prioritizes exit; stop-limit prioritizes price protection but can fail to exit.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>An investor has sold a stock short at RM50 and wants an order designed to initiate repurchasing if the share price rises to RM60. Which order best fits the chapter's discussion?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sell limit at RM60</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Sell stop at RM40</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Buy stop at RM60</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Buy limit at RM40</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter notes that stop orders can also be used to buy securities to limit risk on short sales. Since rising prices hurt the short seller, a buy stop above the current price can trigger an attempt to close the short.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A long position is commonly protected with a sell stop; a short position can be protected with a buy stop.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>An investor buys 100 shares at RM10 each and later sells them at RM10.30. Brokerage commission is RM15 on purchase and RM15 on sale. Ignoring taxes and other charges, what is the net trading profit?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM30 profit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM15 profit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM30 loss</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM0</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Gross capital gain is 100 x (RM10.30 - RM10.00) = RM30. The two brokerage commissions total RM30. Net profit is therefore RM0, illustrating how trading costs can consume apparent gains.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A positive gross trading gain does not necessarily produce a positive net return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>An investor suffers a substantial stock-market loss but the broker remains financially sound and the securities are properly accounted for. The investor demands SIPC reimbursement simply because the investment lost value. Which statement best follows the chapter?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> SIPC guarantees investors against ordinary market declines.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> SIPC protection against certain broker failures is not insurance against poor market performance.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> SIPC covers every loss caused by an inaccurate share-price forecast.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Broker registration converts all investment losses into insured claims.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chapter describes SIPC as protecting against certain broker financial failures, not guaranteeing investors against bad advice, churning or ordinary investment losses. The historical slide's coverage figures should not be treated as automatically current.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Protection against an intermediary's failure is not protection against the securities' market risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>A client and broker are resolving a dispute. Process A involves an informal, voluntary attempt to reach agreement. Process B involves presenting arguments to a panel whose decision is binding if the parties have agreed to binding arbitration. Which classification is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A = arbitration; B = mediation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both processes are ordinary securities trades</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A = mediation; B = arbitration</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Neither process concerns investor protection</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter describes mediation as informal and voluntary, with no binding outcome if agreement fails. Arbitration is a more formal panel-based dispute process, with binding consequences where applicable.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Mediation facilitates agreement; arbitration can result in a binding adjudicated decision.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>An investment advisor is properly registered and holds several professional qualifications. An investor therefore assumes the advisor's recommendations must generate positive returns. Which response is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Registration and qualifications are relevant, but neither guarantees investment competence or profitable outcomes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Registration guarantees that all recommended securities will rise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> An advisor's professional designation eliminates market risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The investor should ignore the advisor's track record and incentives.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The chapter explicitly states that regulatory registration does not guarantee competence. Investors should also consider experience, reputation, track record and whether the advisor understands their circumstances.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Regulatory compliance, professional competence and investment performance are separate concepts.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>A group of investors forms a legal partnership, pools capital, shares research and votes on investment recommendations. Which practice would most improve the quality of its decisions?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Purchase every security recommended by a majority without reviewing evidence.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Concentrate all money in securities promoted by the most confident member.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Increase trade frequency whenever members disagree.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Require members to support recommendations with verifiable evidence, risk analysis and clear investment objectives.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The chapter describes investment clubs as partnerships pooling knowledge and funds to analyze investment opportunities. A disciplined, evidence-based process helps the club make better use of shared research instead of relying only on group confidence.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A collective investment decision is not automatically a well-informed one.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l3p-d6\">PART C - REVISION</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Revision sheet: the distinctions most likely to be tested</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>What to remember</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Descriptive vs analytical</td>\n<td>Historical facts vs interpretation and forecasts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DJIA vs S&amp;P 500</td>\n<td>30-stock average vs broader 500-stock index in the chapter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Russell 1000 vs Russell 2000</td>\n<td>Large-company vs small-company focus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Full-service vs discount broker</td>\n<td>Advice and services vs mainly economical execution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cash vs margin account</td>\n<td>Investor-funded trades vs borrowing privileges</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Odd lot vs round lot</td>\n<td>Fewer than 100 shares vs 100-share units, under the textbook's convention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Market vs limit</td>\n<td>Execution priority vs price control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stop vs stop-limit</td>\n<td>Becomes market order vs becomes limit order when triggered</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Day vs GTC</td>\n<td>Expires at trading-day end vs stays active under the specified terms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mediation vs arbitration</td>\n<td>Negotiated resolution vs formal panel-based resolution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SIPC protection</td>\n<td>Certain broker failures, not ordinary investment losses</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">One question-selection framework</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l3_order_matrix\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>No order gives certainty of both execution and price: each order type picks a side of the trade-off.</figcaption></figure></div><p>When a lecturer gives you an order-execution scenario, use this sequence:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">What does the investor want?\n\n  Trade quickly ───────────────────► Market order\n  Control price ───────────────────► Limit order\n  Trigger an exit attempt ─────────► Stop order\n  Trigger with a price constraint ─► Stop-limit order\n</code></pre>\n<p>The key trade-off is execution certainty versus execution-price control. Neither is guaranteed in all market conditions.</p>\n<p>The most important MCQ lesson in Chapter 3 is that a correct investment idea can still produce a poor outcome when it is based on unreliable information, evaluated against the wrong benchmark, or executed using an inappropriate order.</p>\n<p>Source note: The exam follows your uploaded 2014 U.S.-focused Chapter 3 slides. The questions about Regulation FD, Form 10-K, SIPC and market indexes are textbook-based; historical figures, brokerage practices and specific rules should not automatically be treated as current Malaysian requirements.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l3-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, CFA-style, case competitions, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the chapter&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"3\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Chapter 3 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"3\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which kind of information reports <strong>factual data on the past behaviour</strong> of the economy, a market, an industry or a company?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Analytical information</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Speculative information</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Descriptive information</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Insider information</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Descriptive information is factual data on past behaviour. Analytical information combines current data with projections and recommendations about potential investments.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>What does Regulation FD require?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> That companies pay dividends every year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> That brokers register with the SEC.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> That critical company information be disclosed to investment professionals and the public at the same time.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> That all trades be reported to an exchange within a day.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Regulation FD (fair disclosure) prevents selective disclosure of material information to a privileged group. Broker registration (B) is a different requirement.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which description of a <strong>limit order</strong> matches the chapter?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> An order to buy at or below a specified price, or to sell at or above a specified price; if the limit is not met, the order is not filled.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> An order to buy or sell at the best price available when it is placed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> An order that becomes a market order once a trigger price is reached.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An order that expires at the end of the day.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>B is a market order, C is a stop (stop-loss) order and D describes a day order, which is a time condition rather than a price condition.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why does the chapter warn that online investing removes a \"safety net\"?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because there is no live broker to question or slow down a decision, so mistakes and impulsive trades are easier to make.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because online brokers are always unregulated.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because online trades are slower.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because online investing prohibits limit orders.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The convenience of acting instantly also removes useful friction: a conversation with a broker. The chapter pairs this with warnings about \"free\" advice, frequent trading and margin.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why is the S&amp;P 500 considered more representative of the overall U.S. market than the DJIA?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is calculated using a different calculator.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It includes 500 companies from major industries, versus the DJIA's 30 stocks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It contains only technology stocks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It is published more often.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Breadth is the point: more companies across more industries. The DJIA's 30 high-quality stocks make it popular but narrower.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>What is <strong>street name</strong> registration?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Registering securities in the investor's home address.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Registering securities in the brokerage firm's name while they are held in trust for the client, who remains the beneficial owner.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A way to hide ownership from regulators.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Ownership transferred permanently to the broker.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The broker is the registered holder for convenience, but the investor keeps the economic ownership. Registration convenience is not economic ownership.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A stock trades near RM30. You want to buy but refuse to pay more than RM29.50. Which order fits?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Market order</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Sell stop order at RM29.50</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Limit buy order at RM29.50</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Fill-or-kill market order</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A limit order protects the price: it executes only at RM29.50 or lower. A market order prioritises execution and could fill above RM29.50. A sell stop is for selling.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>An investor buys 1,000 shares at RM5.00 and sells at RM5.30. Commissions are RM30 on each trade. What is the net profit?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM300</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM240</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM270</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM210</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Gross profit = 1,000 x 0.30 = RM300. Total commissions = 2 x 30 = RM60. Net = 300 - 60 = <strong>RM240</strong>.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>You own a stock bought at RM20, now RM25, and place a sell stop at RM22. Overnight bad news makes the stock open at RM18. What most likely happens?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The order sells at exactly RM22.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The order becomes a buy order.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The order is cancelled and no sale occurs.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The stop triggers and becomes a market order, so the sale executes near RM18, below the stop price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The stop price is a trigger, not a guaranteed price. Once triggered the order becomes a market order and fills at the best available price, which here is far lower.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Why can a stop-<strong>limit</strong> order fail to protect an investor on a sharp fall?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because it becomes a market order.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because it becomes a limit order once triggered, and if the price falls through the limit without trading at that level, the order may not fill at all.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because it can only be used for buying.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because it executes before the stop price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A stop-limit gives price control but gives up execution certainty. A plain stop gives execution at an uncertain price.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A broker earns a commission on every trade and recommends that a retiree \"rotate\" their portfolio monthly. What risk is most relevant?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Churning: the broker's incentive (more trades, more commissions) may not align with the client's interest</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Currency risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Event risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Reinvestment risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Excessive trading to generate commissions is churning. Transaction costs are a hurdle every trade must clear, so frequent trading usually reduces net returns.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A balanced fund of Malaysian small-cap stocks returned 12% while the S&amp;P 500 returned 18%. Which analysis is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The fund underperformed by 6 percentage points.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The comparison is unreliable: the S&amp;P 500 does not represent the fund's investment universe, so a small-cap benchmark should be used.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The fund beat the market, because small caps are riskier.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Benchmarks are irrelevant.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Benchmark selection must match the exposure being evaluated. Comparing a Malaysian small-cap portfolio with the S&amp;P 500 is not automatically meaningful.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Evaluate: \"A stop-loss order guarantees that my maximum loss is the difference between my purchase price and the stop price.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Incorrect: the stop becomes a market order when triggered, so a gap can fill well below the stop; a stop-limit may not fill at all.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Correct, but only for day orders.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Incorrect, because stops do not exist for long positions.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Stop price = trigger, not guaranteed sale price. This is among the chapter's most testable ideas.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Evaluate: \"SIPC protection means my investments cannot lose money if my broker fails.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Incorrect: SIPC protects against certain losses from a broker's financial failure, but not against market losses, bad advice or churning.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Correct, because SIPC insures market value without limit.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Incorrect, because SIPC only protects the broker.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Investor protection means protection against certain institutional failures, not against ordinary investment losses. The slides cite historical limits (USD 500,000 for securities, USD 250,000 for cash).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Evaluate: \"Advisors are registered with the SEC, so their advice is reliable.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Incorrect: registration means meeting regulatory requirements; the slides note that no law or regulator guarantees competence, so experience and credentials still matter.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Correct, because registration tests investment skill.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Incorrect, because advisors cannot register.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Licensed does not imply good. A good advisor also asks many questions about the client's goals and circumstances before recommending anything.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Design a sound process for a student wanting to invest in a technology company after seeing an online recommendation.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Buy immediately before the price moves.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Copy a friend's portfolio.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Ask an anonymous forum for a price target and buy the next day.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Read the company's filings, review its financial performance, separate facts from forecasts, compare with competitors and a relevant benchmark, set an entry price, place a limit order and monitor the position.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>It uses primary sources, separates descriptive from analytical information, uses a benchmark and chooses an order type that matches the price condition.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>An investor sees a stock at RM28 but is only willing to buy at RM25 or below, and is happy to wait several weeks. Which order set-up fits best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Market order, day</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Fill-or-kill market order</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sell stop at RM25</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Limit buy at RM25, good-'til-canceled</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The price condition is \"RM25 or lower\" (limit) and the time condition is \"willing to wait\" (GTC). A market order would buy at RM28.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Which design would best protect an investor who has shorted a stock at RM50 against a sharp rise?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Buy stop at RM60</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Sell stop at RM40</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Limit sell at RM60</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No order</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A buy stop above the current price is activated if the price rises to RM60, triggering a purchase that closes the short. The slides note stops can be used to buy stock to limit risk on short positions.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Why might an equal-weighted index and a market-value-weighted index of the same stocks give different returns in the same year?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They use different stocks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Value-weighted indexes ignore market values.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Equal-weighted indexes ignore prices.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> In a value-weighted index the largest companies dominate; in an equal-weighted index every stock counts the same, so a rise in small stocks affects the two differently.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Weighting method determines which stocks drive the index. An index is not neutral: its construction methodology affects what it measures.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A price-weighted average has three stocks priced RM10, RM20 and RM90. Which stock dominates, and what happens if the RM90 stock rises 10% while the others are unchanged?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The RM10 stock dominates; the average rises about 15%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> All stocks are equal; the average is unchanged.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The RM90 stock dominates; the average rises from 40 to 43, about 7.5%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The RM20 stock dominates; the average rises 1%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A price-weighted average is the average of prices: (10 + 20 + 90) / 3 = 40. After the rise: (10 + 20 + 99) / 3 = 43, a 7.5% increase. The highest-priced stock has the biggest influence.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>A bond with a face value of 1,000 and a 6% coupon trades at 950. Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The current yield equals 6% because the coupon is fixed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The yield is below 6%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The yield to maturity is above 6%, because the buyer receives the coupon plus a price gain to par at maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Yield cannot be compared with the coupon rate.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter notes bond yield is total return from buying at the current price and holding to maturity: coupon plus the gain from 950 to 1,000. Coupon ≠ yield.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>An investor places a market order to sell 10,000 shares of a thinly traded stock. Which outcome is the main risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Poor execution: the order is large relative to market depth, so successive fills may occur at progressively worse prices.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The order will never execute.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The order becomes a limit order.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The order expires at day end.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A market order prioritises execution, not price. In a thin market, a large order moves the price against the seller; a limit order would control price at the cost of fill certainty.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A forum user posts a strong buy case and mentions owning the stock. Which is the best first step?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Treat the post as opinion from an interested party, verify its claims against filings and primary evidence, and ask what the poster gains if readers buy.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Buy because the post is detailed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Ignore all online information.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Sell the stock short immediately.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Source incentives matter (pump-and-dump). The skill is to separate evidence from narrative, not to dismiss information.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Why can two broad indexes with similar long-run trends still differ materially over a two-year window, as in Figure 3.5?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One of them is wrong.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Indexes differ only in how often they are published.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They hold different securities and weights, so shocks to particular sectors or large companies affect them by different amounts.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> One excludes dividends by law.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Same market environment ≠ identical index return. Composition and weighting create the divergence.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Behavioural finance suggests investors trade more after gains. Which chapter idea connects most directly?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Mediation versus arbitration</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The definition of the S&amp;P 500</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The warning about frequent trading and day trading: more trades raise costs and error opportunities without necessarily improving returns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Street name registration</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Activity feels like control but costs money. The chapter's stance is that more trades do not automatically mean more return.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A brokerage offers zero commissions but earns money by routing orders to market makers. Evaluate \"free trading means no trading costs\".</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Incomplete: an explicit commission is only one cost; spreads and the execution price also matter, and zero commission may encourage more frequent trading.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Incorrect, because market makers do not exist.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Correct, because spreads are zero.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Transaction costs include commissions, bid-ask spreads and poor execution. Lower visible friction can raise trading frequency, the behavioural issue the chapter flags.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Evaluate: \"A good advisor who charges 1% of assets a year is worth it if performance matches the market.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct, because matching the market is the goal.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct, because fees are never relevant.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Doubtful on returns alone: the fee reduces net return relative to the benchmark unless the advisor adds value through planning, discipline, tax and risk management that the investor could not obtain cheaply.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Incorrect, because advisors cannot match the market.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter notes advice fees and that advice can improve process, not eliminate risk. Value must be weighed against net-of-fee results and services received.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>An analyst argues that because Regulation FD forces simultaneous disclosure, stock prices fully reflect all information instantly. Evaluate.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fully correct.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct because analysts do not exist.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Wrong because FD allows selective disclosure.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Overreach: simultaneous disclosure improves fairness of access, but interpreting information still takes judgment and resources, so prices may adjust unevenly.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Equal access to information is not equal ability to interpret it. The chapter's descriptive-versus-analytical distinction supports this.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>The chapter's brokerage examples, SIPC limits and index lists are from 2014. What is the best research practice?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Quote them as current rules.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Assume they never change.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Delete all numbers.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Use them as the course's reference points and verify current specifics from primary sources before applying them.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Institutional details change; the economic functions (execution, protection, benchmarking) are durable.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Which exam question best distinguishes a student who understands order types from one who memorised definitions?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> \"Define a market order.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> \"What does GTC stand for?\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> \"List four order types.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> \"A holder of a stock bought at RM20 (now RM25) wants downside protection and also wants to avoid selling in a panic gap. Compare a stop and a stop-limit order and recommend one, justifying it.\"</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>It requires applying the execution-certainty versus price-control trade-off to a specific objective, the Bloom Apply-Evaluate level.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
